window.UPGRADE_DOC = {"version": "2026-10 upgrade draft", "sections": [{"id": "title", "num": "", "heading": "Title", "chapter": "Front matter", "kind": "title", "html": "Wellbeing and Responsibility in Neuroliberal Times: News Discourse on AI Chatbots and the Indonesian Case"}, {"id": "abstract", "num": "", "heading": "Abstract", "chapter": "Front matter", "kind": "rich", "html": "<p>This project investigates how news discourse has defined wellbeing in relation to AI chatbots since OpenAI publicly released ChatGPT in November 2022, and how people make sense of these definitions in a setting where they understand wellbeing relationally. Across the Global North and South, people increasingly delegate wellbeing to conversational systems, from general-purpose assistants to companion apps and purpose-built mental health chatbots. Existing research largely measures wellbeing as an outcome of use, or examines how the news frames AI over short periods; it says less about how news defines wellbeing itself, and where it places responsibility for it. Rather than evaluating these tools for safety or efficacy, the study treats wellbeing as a contested category that discourse produces: what counts as wellbeing, who needs it, and what news offers as its remedy.</p><p>Theoretically, the research draws on neuroliberalism, a rationality that governs behaviour by designing the settings in which people choose, and on Giddens’ account of the reflexive project of the self. It proposes that AI companions reproduce the form of the pure relationship while removing its reciprocity, so that users seek wellbeing through the very relationship that reproduces their need for it.</p><p>Methodologically, the study analyses two news corpora through van Dijk’s approach to news as discourse, reading how news assigns responsibility through who acts, who is quoted, what is assumed and what is left unsaid. It traces a corpus of Guardian articles across three stages of emergence, normalisation and containment, and examines Julie Jargon’s ‘Family &amp; Tech’ column in The Wall Street Journal, which relocates the problem into family life. It then turns to Indonesia, where AI chatbots already form part of everyday wellbeing practice, no regulatory framework governs them, and norms of kekeluargaan, hormat, rukun and sungkan organise household life. I interview psychologists and technologists at Riliv and the Indonesian Psychological Association (HIMPSI) and analyse the interviews through reflexive thematic analysis. The study contributes to media studies of AI chatbots, to debates on neuroliberal government, to methods for reading responsibility in news discourse, and to an account of wellbeing beyond Euro-American settings.</p>"}, {"id": "s1-1", "num": "1.1", "heading": "Background", "chapter": "Chapter 1 · Introduction", "kind": "rich", "html": "<p>Across the Global North and South, people increasingly delegate wellbeing to conversational systems. Since OpenAI launched ChatGPT in 2022, conversational AI has expanded rapidly. It now spans general-purpose assistants such as Anthropic’s Claude, companion chatbots such as Character.AI and Replika, mental health chatbots such as Wysa (Lang, 2024), and humanitarian tools such as Karim (Madianou, 2021).</p><p>Media studies research has since examined how the scenario imagined in Spike Jonze’s film Her (2013) has begun to materialise (Depounti and Natale, 2025; Pataranutaporn et al., 2025). People increasingly use relationships with AI chatbots to regulate emotions and manage distress. Even when users do not consider themselves ‘in a relationship’ with an AI companion, routine interactions with chatbots appear to be reshaping expectations of how effortless conversation with other people should be (Kourkoulou, 2024).</p><p>Much of what the public knows about these uses comes through the news. Journalists report the lawsuits, the new safeguards and the stories of users who say a chatbot helped or harmed them, and in doing so they shape what AI chatbot wellbeing is taken to mean. In Indonesia, where apps such as Riliv have offered digital mental health support since 2015, these debates arrive alongside tools already in everyday use. The question this study pursues is therefore not only whether chatbots help, but how wellbeing is being defined around them, and who is made responsible for it.</p>"}, {"id": "s1-2", "num": "1.2", "heading": "The Problem", "chapter": "Chapter 1 · Introduction", "kind": "rich", "html": "<p>Wellbeing is no longer a marginal use of conversational AI but one of its central ones. In October 2025, OpenAI estimated that around 0.15% of ChatGPT users in a given week showed explicit indicators of potential suicidal planning (OpenAI, 2025). With around 800 million weekly users (Wiggers, 2025), that amounts to more than a million people each week. A Common Sense Media survey found that 72% of US teenagers had used an AI companion at least once (Robb and Mann, 2025), and Zao-Sanders (2025) ranked therapy and companionship as the leading use of generative AI.</p><p>Part of the reason people turn to these tools is that professional care is hard to reach. Research in critical psychology links this use to limited access to therapy. In the UK, for example, waiting lists for NHS Talking Therapies can run to weeks, while chatbots offer a sense of ‘private freedom’ that conversations with professionals do not (Fullam, 2025).</p><p>In Indonesia, the gap is wider and takes a different shape. Mental health services remain highly stigmatised (Lukito Setiawan, 2006), and psychiatric care is still largely covered by private insurance rather than by BPJS Kesehatan, the national health insurance scheme (Munira, Liamputtong and Viwattanakulvanid, 2023). In some marginalised families, distress is still managed through pasung, the confinement of a family member living with mental illness (Baklien et al., 2023). Here the family, rather than the individual, is often where distress is held, and sometimes harmfully contained. Where professional care is both scarce and stigmatised, a chatbot that is private and always available may appear to fill a gap that is relational as much as clinical.</p><p>Yet these chatbots are not neutral. They carry a particular understanding of what wellbeing is. Lang’s (2024) ethnography of Wysa shows a chatbot, built around mindfulness and Cognitive Behavioural Therapy (CBT), that defines wellbeing as the capacity to accept distress and moves it away from its social conditions towards individual self-work (pp. 235, 241). This reflects a wider neuroliberal rationality that governs behaviour by designing the settings in which people choose (Whitehead et al., 2018). Read through Giddens’ (1991) account of the reflexive self, companion chatbots also reproduce the form of the ‘pure relationship’ while removing its reciprocity, so that wellbeing is sought through the very relationship that reproduces the need for it.</p><p>This pattern is not new. Conversational AI is not the first technology to arrive wrapped in techno-solutionism that addresses collective struggles individually (Morozov, 2013; Hiland, 2021; Zeavin, 2021). Nor is that solutionism confined to the laboratory; it runs through the household. Connected baby monitors promised parents continuous oversight of their children. Today, parents can subscribe to Troomi, a children’s phone that includes Troodi, a mental health chatbot whose conversations parents can also view (Bernetic, Katic and Candrlic, 2025). Care for a child’s wellbeing is delegated to a chatbot, and supervising that care becomes the parent’s task.</p><p>Research on the problem has largely followed the same individual focus. Reviewing 366 studies of computer-mediated communication and wellbeing published between 2007 and 2021, High et al. (2023) found mostly small and contradictory associations, yet observed that this cautious evidence was drowned out by media headlines and moral panic (p. 422). Journalism is not a neutral relay of research on technology and wellbeing; it shapes what the problem is understood to be.</p>"}, {"id": "s1-3", "num": "1.3", "heading": "Why Now", "chapter": "Chapter 1 · Introduction", "kind": "rich", "html": "<p>The scale described above has not gone unanswered. Across these tools, the debate has shifted from solutionism about expanded access to mental health care towards a more cautious stance (Madianou, 2021; Pataranutaporn et al., 2025; Sedlakova and Trachsel, 2026).</p><p>Harm cases drove much of this shift. In 2024, a 14-year-old boy in the United States died by suicide after months of conversations with a Character.AI chatbot, and the lawsuit his mother brought against the company turned his death into international news (Turkle and Pataranutaporn, 2024).</p><p>Regulation followed. In 2025, Illinois became one of the first US states to bar AI from providing therapy (Wu, 2025). Yet regulation of this kind may contain the tools without containing the practice. Vaile Wright of the American Psychological Association noted that even if states crack down on AI services that present themselves as therapeutic, people are likely to keep turning to chatbots for emotional support, calling it “a very human thing to do” (Wu, 2025).</p><p>Wright’s point shows that containment is itself contested: it is not yet clear who should contain AI chatbot wellbeing, or how. State legislators, professional bodies and the companies themselves all claim a role. OpenAI’s (2025) estimates cited above were published as part of its own effort to strengthen how ChatGPT responds in sensitive conversations. In September 2026, Anthropic’s chief executive, Dario Amodei, went further, calling for the pace of AI development to slow and proposing independent monitoring of models alongside industry-wide and global regulation, a call OpenAI’s Sam Altman publicly endorsed (Kola and Jamali, 2026). Critics, however, read the proposal as an attempt to concentrate control over the technology rather than to make it safer (Kola and Jamali, 2026). The companies building these tools are therefore also positioning themselves to define how they should be contained. That debate, moreover, centres on the risks of frontier models rather than on the everyday use of chatbots for wellbeing. Harm cases, regulation, and corporate self-governance mark a shift from the emergence and normalisation of AI chatbot wellbeing to its containment.</p><p>The meaning of AI chatbot wellbeing is therefore being settled now, while the rules that will govern it are still being written. In the North, the route to AI chatbot wellbeing runs through public debate, harm cases and emerging regulation. In the South, as in Indonesia, it runs through everyday adoption without a governing framework.</p><p>This timing matters because the definition of wellbeing that wins in public discourse will shape what regulation counts as a harm, and whom it holds responsible for preventing it.</p><p>What that definition should be is itself contested, since “what is good for one group may not be good for others” (Madianou, 2021, p. 853). This makes it urgent to examine how wellbeing is being defined in relation to AI chatbots, by whom and for whom, before those definitions harden into policy.</p>"}, {"id": "s1-4", "num": "1.4", "heading": "Previous Research", "chapter": "Chapter 1 · Introduction", "kind": "rich", "html": "<p>Media studies have long examined wellbeing as an individual outcome of media use (Reinecke and Oliver, 2016). High et al.’s (2023) review, the most comprehensive in the field, shows research dominated by individual-level surveys that treat wellbeing as a measurable outcome of use. It excluded communication with machines, closing before conversational AI became widely available, and its authors acknowledge that their Western positioning may obscure social effects that individual-level studies cannot capture (p. 424).</p><p>Research on AI chatbots has begun to fill this gap, but largely by continuing the focus on individual experience. Pataranutaporn et al.’s (2025) computational analysis of 1,506 top-ranked posts in Reddit’s r/MyBoyfriendIsAI community found that companionship mostly emerged unintentionally from functional use, that users far more often named ChatGPT (36.7%) than dedicated companion apps, and that 25.4% of posts reported a clear net benefit against 3.0% reporting net harm. Its sample, however, captures the most engaged members of a self-selected community, and wellbeing appears only as self-reported benefit or harm.</p><p>Approaching from the side of production, Lang’s (2024) ethnography of Wysa’s designers in Bengaluru shows how nonjudgmental listening, acceptance and reframing were encoded into the app, with CBT and mindfulness chosen partly because they were seen as ‘chatbot-able’, and how the designers hoped to move users’ narratives away from socio-economic adversity. Her focus on one app, however, leaves open how such definitions circulate beyond it. Depounti and Natale (2025) offer a linking concept, ‘artificial sociality’, the construction of an appearance of social behaviour in machines, now normalised in general-purpose tools such as ChatGPT. Their agenda, however, centres on the interaction between people and machines rather than on how wider publics come to understand it.</p><p>Critical work on deployment shifts attention from experience to power. Madianou (2021) found that humanitarian chatbots such as Karim fail to deliver the ‘intelligence’ they promise, reduce communication with affected people to box-ticking, and extract value in ways that rework colonial relations of power. Her reading of ‘AI for good’ as an ‘enchantment of technology’ (after Gell, 1992), in which technology is presented as almost magical, explains how such tools gain legitimacy while obscuring these inequalities. Her analysis, however, centres on humanitarian organisations rather than on the public discourse through which that enchantment is produced.</p><p>Studies of news coverage address this public discourse directly, but mostly treat AI as a technology rather than wellbeing as an object. Roe and Perkins (2023) found ‘impending danger’ the most common theme (37%) in 671 UK headlines from early 2023. In Singapore, Tandoc et al.’s (2025) content analysis of 336 articles found coverage dominated by benefits, although risk, regulation and conflict frames grew after ChatGPT’s release. Using topic modelling of 198 US news articles from 2022 to 2024, Sun, Ünlü and Johri (2026) found that coverage of ChatGPT in higher education centred on institutional responses.</p><p>Liao, Rodwell and Porter (2024) come closest to this study’s concern. Combining a frame analysis of 104 news articles on Replika published in 2023 with 16 user interviews, they identified three negative frames: fears about the AI, about its users and about the company. Users accepted the premises of this coverage but rejected its negative tone. These studies show that news shapes the terms of public debate on AI, but they share three limits: short time windows, designs that identify which frames appear rather than how responsibility is allocated within them, and a focus on AI in general, education or romance rather than wellbeing.</p><p>Research on mental health news offers the missing analytical lens but predates conversational AI. Analysing European newspaper and magazine coverage over two weeks in 2018, Van Beveren et al. (2020) identified four constructions of mental health: as danger, as lifestyle, as a unique story and as socially situated. Drawing on Briggs and Hallin’s (2016) framework of biocommunicability, which describes how health news teaches people who knows about health and who must act on it, they showed that the lifestyle construction places responsibility on an ‘enterprising’ individual expected to manage their own mental health. A two-week snapshot, however, cannot capture change over time.</p><p>Research on AI imaginaries in Asia shows how such definitions are shaped by context. Chung’s (2025) analysis of 20 government AI strategies in Singapore, Taiwan and Hong Kong identified a shared techno-developmental imaginary that frames AI as inevitable for national survival. Chung (2026) also argues that critical AI studies remains anchored in Western theory, illustrating this with a Cantonese mental health chatbot whose developers had to interpret indirect and euphemistic expressions of distress. This work, however, analyses state policy rather than news, and none of it addresses Indonesia.</p><p>Across this literature, three limitations stand out. First, wellbeing is either measured as an outcome (High et al., 2023; Pataranutaporn et al., 2025) or defined by designers and states (Lang, 2024; Chung, 2025), rather than examined as a meaning settled in public discourse. Second, studies of AI in the news rely on short time windows and treat AI as the object of coverage, not how that coverage defines wellbeing or allocates responsibility for it. Third, the field remains concentrated on the United States, the United Kingdom and Europe, while Asian research has focused on state policy rather than on the relational settings, such as Indonesian households, into which these framings travel.</p>"}, {"id": "s1-5", "num": "1.5", "heading": "The Research Gap", "chapter": "Chapter 1 · Introduction", "kind": "rich", "html": "<p>The limitations identified above converge on journalism. News is one of the main places where the meaning of AI chatbot wellbeing is publicly settled. Journalism decides which harms count as news, which actors are given voice, which remedies are offered and where responsibility is placed. Chermak (1997) argues that because most people have little direct contact with the processes through which social problems are defined, they depend heavily on news media to understand them, which makes coverage a cheap and effective route for groups pressing their claims to win public support. Those who appear as news sources are therefore not simply informing the public but competing to gain acceptance for their own definitions of a problem and its solutions. Liao, Rodwell and Porter (2024) observed this in coverage of Replika, where users’ voices were taken seriously mainly when they supported critiques of the company.</p><p>Yet no study reviewed here examines how news defines wellbeing itself in relation to AI chatbots. Existing analyses treat AI as the object of coverage and capture it in short windows: five months of UK headlines (Roe and Perkins, 2023), one year of coverage of Replika (Liao, Rodwell and Porter, 2024), or a single comparison before and after ChatGPT (Tandoc et al., 2025). The shifts these studies detect, from benefit towards risk and regulation, suggest that definitions move as the technology moves from emergence to normalisation and containment, but none traces this across the full period since ChatGPT’s release. Van Beveren et al. (2020) offer a lens for asking what counts as wellbeing and who is made responsible for it, but their analysis predates conversational AI.</p><p>Nor has research examined where this coverage places responsibility. Van Beveren et al. (2020) found that lifestyle coverage assigns mental health to the enterprising individual, and Lang (2024) shows designers steering users away from socio-economic explanations of their distress. The household is a further site to which such responsibility may be relocated, as the parental oversight built into Troomi suggests. Yet none of the studies reviewed here examines journalism addressed to families, or how it positions parents as responsible for their children’s wellbeing with AI chatbots.</p><p>Finally, little is known about how such framings travel. They travel to settings where wellbeing is understood relationally rather than individually, such as Indonesian households organised around norms of kekeluargaan, hormat, rukun and sungkan (familyhood, respect, harmony and reserve) (Hermawan and Loo, 2019). Research on Asia has so far examined state strategies rather than the people who build and deliver mental health technology (Chung, 2025), although Chung (2026) and Lang (2024) show that chatbot designers in Hong Kong and India rework imported therapeutic models in local terms. None of this research addresses Indonesia, where AI chatbots are entering everyday wellbeing practice without a governing framework.</p><p>The Northern and Southern routes described in Section 1.3 may nonetheless lead to a similar understanding of wellbeing. Since the COVID-19 pandemic, mental health has increasingly been framed in terms of economic outcomes and addressed through tools that monitor, measure and nudge individuals towards them (Kotouza et al., 2022). Whether news coverage of AI chatbots reproduces this framing, and how those producing mental health technology in Indonesia make sense of it, remain open questions.</p>"}, {"id": "s1-6", "num": "1.6", "heading": "Defining Wellbeing", "chapter": "Chapter 1 · Introduction", "kind": "rich", "html": "<p>The studies reviewed above use ‘wellbeing’ in different ways: as an outcome measured on scales (High et al., 2023), as self-reported benefit and harm (Pataranutaporn et al., 2025), or as something defined in advance by designers (Lang, 2024). This study takes a different position. It approaches the use of AI chatbots for wellbeing not as a clinical intervention to be evaluated for safety or efficacy, but as a social phenomenon that reshapes what wellbeing itself means.</p><p>High et al.’s (2023) review shows how far measurement shapes what wellbeing can mean. It excluded studies that discussed wellbeing without measuring it, studies of short-term mood and studies of satisfaction within a single relationship, and folded social wellbeing into subjective wellbeing as an attribute of the individual (pp. 426–427). Wellbeing became what an instrument could capture in one person. The authors concluded that the field’s conflicting findings may partly reflect the different ways researchers define wellbeing (p. 443), and called for clearer definitions. This study draws a different lesson: if what counts as wellbeing varies with who asks and how, its definition is itself an object of analysis. Two exclusions matter especially here. Momentary relief from distress is precisely what chatbots promise, at any hour (Lang, 2024), and wellbeing located in relationships is the form that norms such as kekeluargaan and rukun presuppose.</p><p>This study therefore treats wellbeing as a contested category whose meaning is produced in discourse. What counts as wellbeing, who is understood to lack it, and what is offered as its remedy are the questions under analysis, not assumptions brought to it. News, in this view, teaches audiences who produces knowledge about wellbeing and who is responsible for acting on it (Van Beveren et al., 2020). Wellbeing is also distinguished from mental health in its clinical sense, which is diagnosed, delivered and regulated within professional systems. Wellbeing names the wider, non-clinical domain of everyday self-care, emotional life and relationships, in which individuals are expected to govern themselves.</p><p>News has redefined wellbeing before. Tracing the term across 1,850 articles in The Guardian and the Daily Mail between 1985 and 2003, Sointu (2005) found that its meaning shifted from the ‘body politic’ to the ‘body personal’. In the mid-1980s, The Guardian used the word mostly for the condition of the nation (p. 259); by the early 1990s, wellbeing had become something individuals were expected to produce for themselves, an obligation presented as a choice (Sointu, 2005, drawing on Rose, 1999). Two findings anticipate this study. Coverage tied workplace wellbeing to productivity and placed responsibility on workers (pp. 264–266), and it addressed women as responsible for wellbeing at home (p. 270), an early form of the relocation of responsibility into family life examined here. Sointu’s analysis ends in 2003. This study asks what happens to the same trajectory once wellbeing is delegated to a machine.</p><p>Lee, Kubzansky and VanderWeele (2021) caution against calling everything wellbeing, and distinguish objective wellbeing from subjective psychological wellbeing; this study is concerned with the latter. In particular, it takes as its object the lifestyle construction identified by Van Beveren et al. (2020), in which mental wellbeing and happiness are pursued through self-care and individual choice, while remaining attentive to other constructions, such as danger and social context.</p>"}, {"id": "s1-7", "num": "1.7", "heading": "Research Questions", "chapter": "Chapter 1 · Introduction", "kind": "rich", "html": "<p>This study therefore shifts the focus from the effects of AI chatbots on individual wellbeing to how news discourse defines wellbeing in relation to these tools. It traces how public news and family journalism frame wellbeing over time, and where they place responsibility for it. It then turns to Indonesia, where AI chatbots are already part of everyday wellbeing practice, no law or regulation governs them, and responsibility is often shared rather than carried by the individual alone. In this context, the study asks how the psychologists and technologists who build and deliver these tools understand wellbeing, and what role they see for themselves in shaping it.</p><p>RQ1. To what extent, and in what ways, has news discourse redefined wellbeing through the emergence of AI chatbots across the stages of emergence, normalisation and containment (2023 to 2026): what has counted as wellbeing, who has been understood to need it, and what has been offered as its remedy?</p><p>RQ2. How does news discourse relocate the problem of AI chatbot wellbeing from public regulation into private life, such as the household, the workplace and personal relationships, and where does it place responsibility?</p><p>RQ3. How do psychologists and technologists producing mental health technology understand wellbeing and responsibility for users’ wellbeing, and what role do they see themselves playing in the future governance of AI chatbots?</p>"}, {"id": "s1-8", "num": "1.8", "heading": "Research Design Overview", "chapter": "Chapter 1 · Introduction", "kind": "rich", "html": "<p>This study uses a sequential qualitative design in three strands, one for each research question. The two news strands come first and establish how wellbeing is defined in public and in family journalism; the interviews then follow those definitions into Indonesia. Chapter 4 sets out each strand in detail.</p><p>First, for RQ1, it analyses articles from The Guardian retrieved with the search string “AI chatbot and wellbeing”, from 1 January 2023, a month after ChatGPT’s public release in November 2022, to the end of 2026; 86 articles have been identified so far (see Section 4.4). Second, for RQ2, it analyses Julie Jargon’s ‘Family &amp; Tech’ column in The Wall Street Journal, which reports on how technology shapes family life and places responsibility for wellbeing on the household. This corpus comprises 20 articles published between 1 June 2023 and 10 October 2026, with collection remaining open until 31 December 2026. Both corpora are analysed through van Dijk’s (1983) approach to news as discourse.</p><p>Third, for RQ3, the study turns to Indonesia through online interviews conducted on Microsoft Teams. Participants are drawn from Riliv, an award-winning Indonesian mental health support app running since 2015, and from the Indonesian Psychological Association (HIMPSI), the professional body that advises Riliv and regulates its psychologists. They comprise Riliv’s two co-founders, its chief executive and chief operating officer; up to five Riliv psychologists who work with families and young people, referred by the co-founders, who take part first in a focus group discussion and then in individual in-depth interviews; and a representative of HIMPSI’s Department of National Policy Synergy (Bidang Sinergitas Kebijakan Nasional), reached through Riliv. Psychologists are identified only by initials, and no client’s name, age or gender is recorded. The interviews are analysed through reflexive thematic analysis (Braun and Clarke, 2006, 2019).</p>"}, {"id": "s1-9", "num": "1.9", "heading": "Positionality Statement", "chapter": "Chapter 1 · Introduction", "kind": "rich", "html": "<p>I approach this study as an Indonesian researcher from a working-class background, funded in full by the Indonesia Endowment Fund for Education (LPDP), a government scholarship that expects its recipients to contribute their work back to Indonesia. The study is therefore written with Indonesian concerns in view, and not only debates in the United States and Europe. My relationship to the field predates this research. In 2015–2016, before beginning a Master’s degree in Newcastle in 2016, I volunteered at Riliv, supporting its public relations and its recruitment of psychologists. Since 2021 I have taught at my affiliated university in Surabaya, and since 2023 I have been the mother of a young daughter. These roles make the neuroliberal understanding of wellbeing traced in Chapter 3, in which each person is expected to manage their own mind, more than an object of analysis. It is the understanding my students are growing up with, and one my daughter is likely to inherit. This is part of why RQ2 attends to the family, where responsibility for a child’s wellbeing is increasingly placed, and where parents, myself included, are asked to carry it.</p><p>This position shapes both access and interpretation. My earlier work with Riliv means that I approach its founders as a known contact rather than a stranger, and Riliv has agreed to take part in the study. Its founders also provide the route to its psychologists and to HIMPSI (Section 4.6). As an Indonesian speaker who shares many of the relational norms discussed in Chapters 2 and 3, I can conduct interviews in Indonesian, follow what participants leave unsaid, and recognise when sungkan or hormat may shape what they are willing to say. As a communication graduate, I can also read the news corpora with an understanding of how organisations present themselves to the public. The same closeness, however, carries risks. Participants who know me, or who know of my earlier role, may present Riliv favourably or hesitate to criticise it, and I may take for granted norms that an outsider would question. Reflexive thematic analysis treats the researcher’s position as a resource to be examined rather than a bias to be removed (Braun and Clarke, 2019). I will therefore keep reflexive memos throughout the analysis, record how my relationship with each participant group may have shaped the interviews, and report the recruitment route through Riliv as a limitation (Section 4.7).</p>"}, {"id": "s1-10", "num": "1.10", "heading": "Proposed Contributions", "chapter": "Chapter 1 · Introduction", "kind": "rich", "html": "<p>This study makes four linked contributions.</p><p>First, empirically, it tracks how news discourse redefined wellbeing across emergence, normalisation and containment from 2023 to 2026, showing what counted as wellbeing at each stage, who was understood to need it, and what was offered as its remedy. As noted in Section 1.4, media studies of AI chatbots have examined higher education, intimate user communities and romantic framings, but not wellbeing as the object of news discourse.</p><p>Second, theoretically, it extends neuroliberalism (Whitehead et al., 2018) by showing how public discourse allocates responsibility for wellbeing ahead of regulation, reshaping what government can do. It also places Giddens’ (1991) reflexive project of the self in dialogue with the relational norms of familyhood, respect, harmony and reserve (Hermawan and Loo, 2019), asking how individualised models of wellbeing travel into settings where responsibility is shared.</p><p>Third, methodologically, it shows how responsibility is assigned through the grammar of news: who acts, who is quoted, what is assumed and what is left unsaid (van Dijk, 1983). It compares two types of journalism, public news and family journalism, over the same period.</p><p>Fourth, it offers a contextual account of production and governance from Indonesia, where no framework yet governs conversational systems.</p>"}, {"id": "s2-1", "num": "2.1", "heading": "The object: three categories of AI chatbot wellbeing", "chapter": "Chapter 2 · Research Context", "kind": "rich", "html": "<p>Before describing the setting of this research, it is necessary to distinguish the three broad categories of AI chatbots now used for wellbeing in everyday life. They differ in what they were built to do, in how tightly their conversations are constrained, and in the commitments their providers make to users. News discourse often does not observe these distinctions, and this research examines what follows from treating them as instances of a single thing.</p><p>The first category is the labelled or purpose-built chatbot, designed and marketed explicitly for mental health. Wysa, developed by Touchkin eServices in Bengaluru, and Woebot, developed by Stanford-affiliated clinicians in the United States, both operate through structured, scripted conversations grounded in cognitive behavioural therapy and mindfulness (Fullam, 2025; Lang, 2024). Wysa is presented to users as a gender-neutral penguin that acts as a digital coach or happiness buddy and delivers daily check-ins through fixed, pre-written conversations. Fullam&#x27;s study of a comparable application traces the same architecture at the level of design: user inputs are identified through keywords and tags, and responses are selected from a limited set of responses. The conversational range of these systems is narrow by intention, and what counts as a mental health concern is settled in advance by the framework the designers adopt. These services typically operate on a freemium subscription model and are also sold to employers and health systems, and Wysa has obtained NHS approval for talking therapies (Touchkin eServices, 2026).</p><p>The second category is the general-purpose generative chatbot, built for open-ended conversation rather than for therapy, but increasingly approached by users as someone to talk to about their problems. It includes OpenAI&#x27;s ChatGPT, Anthropic&#x27;s Claude, Google&#x27;s Gemini and Meta&#x27;s Llama. Tan et al. (2025) tested seven leading models against twelve psychotherapy-related tasks across cases of depression, anxiety and stress. No model produced consistently high-quality responses, performance varied across structured and integrative tasks, and the strongest responses showed clinical humility, maintained therapeutic boundaries and treated therapy as collaborative, qualities the models achieved only unevenly. Their conclusion is that current large language models are more promising as supportive tools for clinicians than as therapeutic applications. Unlike the labelled chatbot, these systems make no therapeutic claim, offer no fixed set of responses, and carry safety guardrails that differ by provider and change with each update cycle. What they will say to a person in distress is therefore not fixed, and is not disclosed.</p><p>The third category is the companion platform, such as Replika and Character.AI, built to sustain an ongoing relationship rather than to treat a condition. No therapeutic framing is offered, yet these platforms support the most intensive emotional use of the three. Laestadius et al. (2024) document the forms of dependence that develop among Replika users, and Pataranutaporn et al. (2025) find users of companion communities crediting these systems with psychological healing while also grieving when a system update alters the character they have come to rely on. Companion platforms create real psychological benefits, but because they were never marketed as therapy, it&#x27;s unclear what responsibility the provider has to the user.</p><p>These categories are not equivalent, and the difference is not only technical. The labelled chatbot offers a deliberately narrow set of responses shaped by psychological frameworks and corporate ethics. The generative chatbot offers a wide range of responses that the user must navigate alone. The companion platform offers a relationship that no clinical framework governs. This research holds all three in view, because it is the category most distant from therapy that has produced the most therapeutic attachment, and because the public discourse analysed in the chapters that follow moves between them without marking the difference.</p>"}, {"id": "s2-2", "num": "2.2", "heading": "The Regulatory Landscape", "chapter": "Chapter 2 · Research Context", "kind": "rich", "html": "<p>AI chatbots used for wellbeing do not fit neatly into any existing set of rules. They are rarely treated as medical devices, they are not clinical services, and the codes that govern psychologists do not apply to them. Section 1.3 showed that the question of who should contain these systems remains contested. This section examines the rules that do exist and why each falls short.</p><p>The first gap concerns medical devices. The United States Food and Drug Administration has authorised more than 1,500 AI tools for health care, but most are narrow systems, such as software that spots fractures on an X-ray (SwissMed AI, 2026). A chatbot that holds open conversations about distress is a different kind of object. Researchers have asked the FDA to set limits on chatbots used for therapy (Singh et al., 2025), but its device categories were not designed for them. Where chatbots have entered medical regulation, they have done so by narrowing their claims. Wysa holds an FDA Breakthrough Device Designation for patients with chronic pain, depression and anxiety in orthopaedic care, while remaining classified elsewhere as a self-help and wellbeing tool (Lang, 2024). It has also been approved for use in NHS talking therapies (O’Brien, 2023). These cases show that a tightly scripted app can be regulated, but the open-ended chatbots that cause most public concern cannot be handled the same way.</p><p>The second response comes from the professions. Laws such as Illinois’ 2025 ban on AI therapy (Section 1.3) regulate therapists and employers rather than chatbots, because those are the actors the state already licenses (Wu, 2025). Regulation of this kind controls who may provide chatbot support, not who uses it, or where.</p><p>Both responses rest on limited evidence. A major review of 35 studies found that chatbots reduced distress and depression but did not significantly improve wellbeing (Li et al., 2023). Fewer than half of those studies included any safety measure, such as a route to a human professional in a crisis, and only five tested the kind of generative chatbot most people now use. Regulators are therefore being asked to govern systems that have barely been studied.</p><p>Existing laws may also target the wrong kind of harm. Teo, Mann and Jurcys (2025) argue that rules such as the EU AI Act assume vulnerable users can be identified in advance, for example by age or diagnosis. With companion chatbots, vulnerability often develops gradually through the relationship itself. The same human-like qualities that make these systems comforting also lead users to trust and depend on them, and no simple ban resolves that tension.</p><p>Outside the United States and Europe, regulation has played a different role. Comparing AI strategies in Singapore, Taiwan and Hong Kong, Chung (2025) finds that governments use regulation less to restrain the technology than to perform responsibility while sustaining developmental optimism. Taiwan frames AI governance as a defence of democratic and cultural sovereignty; Hong Kong’s main regulatory statement concerns AI in financial markets; and Singapore uses its reputation for AI ethics to attract global capital. None of these frameworks addresses mental health chatbots directly.</p><p>These gaps follow a pattern. Regulation reaches the device and the licensed professional, but not the everyday, non-clinical use in which most people turn to chatbots for wellbeing. That domain is left to self-governance: to users, to parents, and to the companies’ own safeguards. This is the space where, as Chapter 1 argued, responsibility is allocated before regulation arrives. The next section turns to Indonesia, where even these partial rules do not yet exist.</p>"}, {"id": "s2-3", "num": "2.3", "heading": "The Indonesian Context", "chapter": "Chapter 2 · Research Context", "kind": "rich", "html": "<p>Clinical psychiatry arrived in Indonesia with the Dutch colonial administration, which built the country’s first psychiatric hospitals. By 1949, four years after independence, Indonesia inherited four psychiatric hospitals and a system that separated patients by race (Dahlia and Marthoenis, 2020). Dutch practice distinguished ‘European’ from ‘indigenous’ patients and assigned each a different regime: European patients received rest, air and sun cures, while indigenous patients were directed into occupational labour, including agricultural work, on the presumption that their ‘mental sickness’ was laziness. Psychiatry entered Indonesia as an instrument of classification before it became a service of care.</p><p>This inheritance is one of several histories behind what Susanti, Elfira and Yulianti (2026) describe as silent suffering, which this study extends to mean a culturally and politically conditioned reluctance to speak of distress in clinical terms. The relational norms introduced in Chapter 1 sustain it: sungkan discourages burdening others with one’s troubles, and rukun places the harmony of the group above the expression of individual difficulty. A second history lies in the tension between indigenous healing and secular rationality. The statesman and teacher Tan Malaka, writing in the 1920s and 1940s, argued that “where capitalism arises, and lays down roots, there a healthy mind and rationalism begins to grow, and superstitious belief begin gradually to disappear” (Crawford, 2019). His 1943 book Madilog circulated among young Indonesians during the Japanese occupation and called for an education in class power and worldview rather than in ideas about the afterlife and superstition (Crawford, 2019). Tan Malaka did not write about mental health, but his framing positioned spiritual idioms, including spiritual idioms of distress, as marks of backwardness: something to leave behind rather than speak. A third history lies in the New Order, which after 1965 made silence a condition of survival. The mass killings of alleged communists, and the stigma attached to their families, taught Indonesians that speaking of suffering could be dangerous (Heryanto, 2006). The pasung described in Chapter 1 is silent suffering in its most extreme form. It matters to this study because it is containment in the most literal sense, carried out by families where services are absent (Dahlia and Marthoenis, 2020).</p><p>The state entered the field late. Mental health provisions appeared in the 2009 Health Law (UU No. 36/2009), and a dedicated Mental Health Law followed only in 2014 (UU No. 18/2014). Both were repealed when the omnibus Health Law of 2023 (UU No. 17/2023) revoked eleven existing laws and folded mental health into a single health framework. Psychology gained state recognition as a profession only in 2022, through the Law on Psychology Education and Services (UU No. 23/2022), which made HIMPSI the sole official professional body, with authority to recommend and issue psychologists’ registration and practice licences. AI, by contrast, is governed mainly through a national strategy and non-binding ethical guidance, alongside the 2022 Personal Data Protection Law. None of these addresses chatbots used for wellbeing. Responsibility for people with mental illness has therefore continued to rest largely with families (Mahwati and Hasibuan, 2025).</p><p>Digital media is now redistributing that responsibility. On social media, Indonesian users “actively construct narratives that normalise vulnerability, promote empathy, and encourage shared responsibility for well-being” (Susanto, Sukendar and Licia, 2026, p. 800), although stigmatising expressions persist, which indicates that the shift remains partial and contested. Research on AI chatbots for wellbeing in Indonesia, meanwhile, leans towards techno-optimism, emphasising their capacity to offer immediate, anonymous and continuous support, including to users in remote communities (Arief, Hamzah and Azham, 2025). This optimism takes concrete form in applications designed for the Indonesian market: Savandha et al. (2026) trace the emergence of digital mental health platforms as a response to the country’s acute shortage of mental health professionals.</p><p>These developments explain the choice of interviewees. Riliv, running since 2015, is one of the platforms through which digital care entered Indonesian everyday life, while HIMPSI, under the 2022 law, licenses the psychologists who work within it. They sit at the point where silent suffering, family responsibility and digital self-care now meet, and where the definitions of wellbeing circulating in Western news are most likely to be reworked.</p>"}, {"id": "s3-1", "num": "3.1", "heading": "AI chatbots for wellbeing: an old techno-solutionism promise", "chapter": "Chapter 3 · Literature Review", "kind": "rich", "html": "<p>AI chatbots for wellbeing arrive with an imagined future already attached. Before any user opens the app, its designers have decided what kind of problem distress is and what kind of care can answer it. Lang’s (2024) ethnography of Wysa, introduced in Section 1.4, shows this at work: CBT and mindfulness were chosen partly because they were ‘chatbot-able’, and the designers hoped to steer users’ narratives away from socio-economic adversity and towards what could be worked on alone. The imaginary behind Wysa is therefore more than a promise of access. It decides in advance that distress is the kind of problem a conversation with software can address.</p><p>How this imaginary became persuasive is a story of techno-solutionism, which Morozov (2013) describes as the habit of recasting complex social conditions as problems with a technological fix. Hiland (2021) traces how this habit took hold in mental health care. In therapy tech, a shortage of affordable care was reframed as a gap that apps could close, an agenda advanced at industry events and conferences and financed by investors. The ‘mental health crisis’ became both the justification for these tools and their market. The AI chatbot is its most ambitious expression, promising not only to connect people to care but to deliver it, at any hour and at almost no cost.</p><p>Mediated therapy, however, is not new. Zeavin (2021) shows that therapy has been delivered at a distance for as long as it has existed, from Freud’s letters to radio advice programmes, telephone crisis lines and ELIZA, the 1960s program that imitated a therapist. Each new medium was greeted as a way to extend care beyond the consulting room, and each raised the same doubts about intimacy, safety and who was really listening. In this context, the AI chatbot is one more step in a long history rather than a rupture. What has changed is where the listener sits. In earlier forms of distance therapy, a human remained at the other end of the line. In the chatbot, the other end is the system itself.</p><p>That shift matters for how wellbeing is understood. Sointu (2005) found that in British newspapers between 1985 and 2003, wellbeing moved from the ‘body politic’, the condition of the nation, to the ‘body personal’, something each individual was expected to produce for themselves. The AI chatbot extends this movement. Wellbeing is now pursued in a private conversation with a machine, and its success is measured by how the individual feels afterwards. Section 3.2 returns to Sointu’s trajectory in detail. Here, it is enough to note that the chatbot does not begin the individualisation of wellbeing. It inherits it, and gives it a new form.</p><p>What this inherited individualisation looks like in everyday use is visible in users’ own accounts. Pataranutaporn et al.’s (2025) analysis of the r/MyBoyfriendIsAI community (see Section 1.4) found that companionship mostly emerged out of functional use, and that far more posts reported a net benefit than a net harm. Many posters described their AI partner as part of how they now look after their mental health: a place to process the day, to regulate difficult feelings and to feel understood. On these users’ own terms, the chatbot works as a mental health practice.</p><p>Yet the practice these posts describe runs in the opposite direction to therapy. Therapy, whatever its school, aims to return the person to the world: to make them more grounded in their circumstances and better able to sustain relationships with others. The AI partner offers something different. It is endlessly available, consistently affirming and shaped around a single user, and in that sense it builds a bubble rather than a bridge. The comfort it provides depends on keeping out the friction, disagreement and reciprocity that relationships with other people involve. The user feels better without necessarily becoming more connected, and the relief is found inside the relationship with the machine rather than beyond it.</p><p>Giddens (1991) helps explain why such a bubble is wanted. He links the rise of therapy to high modernity, the present stage of modern society, in which tradition no longer settles who one should be and crisis feels constant. People are kept going, he argues, by a ‘protective cocoon’ of basic trust that filters out these dangers, and therapy is one of the expert systems, bodies of professional knowledge such as medicine or psychology, that help individuals rebuild that trust when it fails. The AI chatbot answers the same need but offers the cocoon without the reflexive work: a shelter from uncertainty that does not ask the user to re-engage with it. It turns the therapeutic promise inward, producing the loop described in Section 1.2, which Section 3.3 examines in detail.</p>"}, {"id": "s3-2", "num": "3.2", "heading": "The trajectories of wellbeing research", "chapter": "Chapter 3 · Literature Review", "kind": "rich", "html": "<p>Research on wellbeing has followed more than one trajectory, and each makes a different version of the problem visible. This section traces two of them. The first, from media psychology, treats wellbeing as an outcome that media use can raise or lower, and has recently moved towards the brain. The second, from social theory and anthropology, treats wellbeing as a political category that carries assumptions about what a person is. The first supplies the language in which AI chatbots are now designed and discussed. The second supplies the questions this study asks of that language.</p><p>In media studies, the field was given its shape by Reinecke and Oliver’s (2016) Routledge Handbook of Media Use and Well-Being, which aligned the study of media with ‘positive psychology’ and its shift from pathology to positive experience (Seligman and Csikszentmihalyi, 2000). Wellbeing is divided between two schools. Hedonic wellbeing, following Diener, means positive affect, the absence of negative affect and satisfaction with life. Eudaimonic wellbeing, following Ryff and Self-Determination Theory, adds personal growth, purpose and the satisfaction of needs for autonomy, competence and relatedness, the need to feel connected to others. Media can serve both: they repair mood, but they can also offer meaning and belonging.</p><p>Some of Reinecke and Oliver’s observations align with this study. They treat the relationship between media and wellbeing as reciprocal, as users select media to regulate their mood, and they recognise that this can turn into a ‘downward spiral’ in which media used as an escape feed the stress they were meant to relieve. This is close to the loop described in Section 3.1. They also concede that research on cultural difference is scarce.</p><p>The same framework, however, locates both problem and solution in the individual. Whether media help or harm depends on individual competencies: self-control, media literacy and, for children, parental mediation. The ideal that closes their review is ‘the fully functioning individual’. Platform design or the absence of care elsewhere appear only as context, and responsibility falls on the user, and for children on the parent, which is the allocation this study traces in news discourse.</p><p>This individual focus deepens in the handbook’s turn to neuroscience. Weber, Huskey and Craighead (2016) take up flow, a state of focused, rewarding absorption that arises when a task’s challenge matches a person’s skill (pp. 183–184), and seek to explain how the brain produces it. Communication theories, they argue, have treated the brain as a black box (p. 185). They are sceptical of self-report, noting that ‘sophisticated statistics do not make self-report data more valid’ (p. 189), and look ahead to using neural responses to predict how enjoyable media content will be across a population (p. 189). Self-regulation becomes a balance between control from the prefrontal cortex and impulses from deeper brain regions, and ‘failure of self-regulation contributes to addiction’ (pp. 191–192).</p><p>This matters for Section 3.4. Once wellbeing is defined as a brain state, it can be measured without asking the person and designed for in advance, and harm appears as a failure of individual self-regulation rather than a feature of the environment. This is the knowledge base on which neuroliberal government draws (Whitehead et al., 2018). When engagement with an AI chatbot turns into dependence, this account locates the failure in the user’s self-control rather than in the system’s design.</p><p>A second trajectory questions what this account takes for granted. Corsín Jiménez (2008) traces how wellbeing became ‘a standard currency in economic and political models of welfare and development’ (p. 2), used to ‘telescope the individual into the social’ (p. 4). Every way of measuring wellbeing, he argues, shapes how we imagine the persons in our theories, and ‘all theories of well-being carry a social theory within them’ (p. 11). In the development programmes his volume reviews, health problems become ‘the problems of “individuals”’, and the structural conditions behind them are rarely considered (p. 18).</p><p>The most developed alternative is relational wellbeing, which White (2010; 2015; 2017) built from development research in India and Zambia. White defines wellbeing as ‘a social process with material, relational, and subjective dimensions’ (2010, p. 158), located not in the individual but in relationships: ‘wellbeing inheres in the dance, it is not the property of individual dancers’ (2015, p. 11). Her critique reaches into psychology. Even where it stresses relatedness, as Self-Determination Theory does, ‘wellbeing is still seen as the property of an individual’ (2015, p. 9). A relational approach also changes where responsibility lies, asking people to think relationally ‘rather than to assume all responsibility for “their” wellbeing belongs at the individual level’ (2015, p. 15).</p><p>These critiques align with the position taken in Section 1.6, that wellbeing is a contested category whose meaning is produced rather than found. Relational wellbeing also offers a vocabulary for the Indonesian case: a wellbeing located in relationships is closer to kekeluargaan and rukun than to the hedonic and eudaimonic scales of media psychology. It also clarifies what is at stake in the chatbot, which reproduces relatedness as an individual need and meets it inside one relationship with a machine. Neither Corsín Jiménez nor White addresses media or technology, however. This study works between the two trajectories, reading the psychological definitions of wellbeing that designers, journalists and regulators now use with the question both critiques raise: what theory of the person does each definition carry, and who is made responsible for its wellbeing?</p>"}, {"id": "s3-3", "num": "3.3", "heading": "The reflexive self, artificial sociality and the loop", "chapter": "Chapter 3 · Literature Review", "kind": "rich", "html": "<p>Section 3.1 described a loop in which the chatbot sought for comfort keeps users needing it. Section 3.2 showed that the dominant research tradition explains this through the individual, as a failure of self-regulation. This section asks instead why the relationship takes this form, through theories of the self. It begins with Giddens’s (1991) reflexive self and Hacking’s looping effects, as developed by Kvist Lindholm and Wickström (2020), then turns to artificial sociality (Depounti and Natale, 2025), therapeutic culture (Illouz, 2008) and Turkle’s (2011) argument that we now expect more from technology and less from each other. It closes by setting these individual selves against a Javanese self, formed through sungkan, rukun and hormat (Magnis-Suseno, 1984), for whom the relationship comes first.</p><p>For Giddens (1991), the self in late modernity is a reflexive project that must be continuously narrated and revised, drawing on expert systems whose knowledge filters back into everyday life. Intimacy takes a matching form in the pure relationship, a bond sustained only while it delivers enough satisfaction to each partner. Companion chatbots reproduce this form while removing its reciprocity (Section 1.2): the machine never tires or withdraws, so the relationship’s condition is met indefinitely by one side. The chatbot is also an expert system. Many are built on therapeutic frameworks such as CBT, so the reflexive self that turns to them is supplied with the terms in which to understand itself.</p><p>Hacking’s notion of looping effects explains what follows. Classifications, he argues, ‘make up people’, and ‘classifying changes people, but the changed people cause classifications themselves to be redrawn’ (Hacking, 2004, p. 279, cited in Kvist Lindholm and Wickström, 2020, p. 29). Kvist Lindholm and Wickström (2020) show this in young people’s mental health. School programmes increasingly read everyday troubles as symptoms, a ‘psychiatrisation’ of ordinary life, in which everyday problems are seen through psychiatric labels (p. 28). Yet the 15-year-olds they interviewed used ‘anxiety’ and ‘depression’ as cultural rather than diagnostic categories, relocating their causes to school pressure and family conflict (p. 33), and defined wellbeing as having friends and family they could rely on (p. 30). AI chatbots and the news both classify: a chatbot sorts users by risk, and news names new kinds of people, such as the teenager ‘seeking out AI friends’. News discourse is therefore one place where this loop runs, and where the meaning of AI chatbot wellbeing is both fixed and contested.</p><p>That a machine can occupy the place of a partner depends on what Depounti and Natale (2025) call artificial sociality, the construction of an appearance of social behaviour in machines, now normalised in general-purpose tools such as ChatGPT. This helps explain why Pataranutaporn et al. (2025) found companionship emerging out of functional use. Users need not be deceived. Natale (2021) describes a ‘banal deception’ in which people know they are dealing with a machine but go along with its social cues because doing so makes the interaction easier. The relationship is knowingly entered into with a system designed to appear social.</p><p>What users seek within it is shaped by therapeutic culture. Illouz (2008) argues that therapy has become a main code through which modern selves understand their emotions and lives, built on a narrative of self-realisation that depends on suffering: one must recognise one’s pain, name it and work on it. The AI chatbot automates this narrative, inviting users to name and reframe what they feel at any hour. It is not an alternative to therapeutic culture but its most accessible form.</p><p>Turkle (2011) identifies the cost: sociable machines offer ‘the illusion of companionship without the demands of friendship’, and lower the value placed on the harder work of human relationships. This is the bubble described in Section 3.1. The loop holds because each turn is reasonable on its own terms: the reflexive self seeks a relationship that satisfies, artificial sociality supplies a partner that always does, therapeutic culture locates wellbeing in managing one’s feelings, and the machine makes that work easier alone than with others.</p><p>These theories assume a self that is first an individual and only then related to others. Javanese ethics, as Magnis-Suseno (1984) describes it, begins from the other end, with two principles: rukun, the obligation to maintain harmony by preventing conflict from surfacing openly, and hormat, the obligation to show respect according to each person’s place. Both are learned in the family through wedi (fear), isin (shame) and sungkan, a respectful reserve before superiors or those one does not know well (H. Geertz, 1961). Where Giddens’s reflexive self asks who it is, the self of rukun and hormat asks where it stands and what it owes. Its wellbeing is closer to relational wellbeing (Section 3.2).</p><p>This changes how the chatbot can be read. For a self bound by rukun and hormat, it may offer a listener outside the web of obligation altogether. Disclosing distress to family can disturb harmony, and disclosing it to an elder or professional can breach the reserve that sungkan requires. A chatbot carries neither risk. It may therefore appeal not because it answers an individual need, as media psychology assumes, but because it resolves a relational one: it makes speaking possible without unsettling the relationships that speaking would put at risk.</p><p>The same feature may close the loop in a new form. Distress voiced to a machine can stay outside the family indefinitely, extending the silent suffering described in Section 2.3. The chatbot also brings classifications such as anxiety and self-care into a setting where distress has been understood in other terms, and these may be redrawn rather than absorbed (Kvist Lindholm and Wickström, 2020), a question for RQ3. This account must be held with care. Magnis-Suseno describes an ethical ideal rather than how every Javanese person lives, the language of harmony has been used politically by the New Order state (Bourchier, 2015), and Javanese norms do not stand for Indonesia as a whole. They serve here as a contrast that shows the individualism of the dominant theories. Section 3.4 asks who governs the setting in which the self turns to the machine.</p>"}, {"id": "s3-4", "num": "3.4", "heading": "Neuroliberalism as theoretical framework", "chapter": "Chapter 3 · Literature Review", "kind": "rich", "html": "<p>What it means to be a good citizen has changed, and with it what it means to be well. In the welfare state after the Second World War, citizenship was a set of rights and duties: Marshall (1950) described the extension of civil, political and social rights, the last promising economic welfare and security. In return for work and taxes, the state protected citizens from illness, unemployment and old age. Wellbeing was largely collective, and in the mid-1980s The Guardian still used the word mostly for the condition of the nation (Sointu, 2005). In industrial capitalism, as Kinnari, Laalo and Silvennoinen (2025) note, ‘personal traits, emotional and social skills, or mental capacities were not considered important for productivity’ (p. 5).</p><p>Today’s good citizen is defined differently. Since the 1990s, Kinnari et al. (2025) argue, the entrepreneurial self has become ‘the proper mindset for citizens’ (p. 2). In cognitive capitalism, people’s thinking and emotional abilities are their main economic assets, and human capital, meaning skills and abilities treated as economic assets, expands to ‘attitudes, motivations, emotions and personality traits’ (p. 5). OECD and EU policy produce an ideal they call the ‘ability-capital machine’, who must master ever more competences, including ‘controlling impulses and emotions’ (p. 8). Wellbeing becomes one of them: ‘individual well-being helps build economic, human, social and natural capital’ (OECD, 2019, cited on p. 15). The good citizen manages their own mind well enough to remain productive and well.</p><p>Neuroliberalism names the form of government that corresponds to this citizen. Isin (2004) first proposed the term, arguing that the neoliberal image of a ‘sufficient, calculating, responsible, autonomous, and unencumbered’ subject was always accompanied by a neurotic citizen ‘who governs itself through responses to anxieties and uncertainties’ (pp. 217, 223). Whitehead et al. (2019) develop it around the behavioural sciences, as ‘systems of government that are primarily characterized by the mobilization of novel cognitive strategies, emotions, and precognitive affects as a way of securing preferred forms of social conduct while ostensibly supporting liberal orthodoxies of freedom’ (p. 633). In other words, it shapes behaviour by working on habits, emotions and reactions that happen before conscious thought, while appearing to leave people free to choose. Importantly, they extend it beyond states to ‘corporations, and non-governmental organizations’ (p. 633). Isin’s version governs through anxiety, Whitehead et al.’s through the design of choice. Its main tools are positive psychology, which hands ‘the responsibility of being happy’ to the individual, and behavioural economics, which steers irrationality towards productivity (Kinnari et al., 2025, pp. 5–6). This is the knowledge base traced in Section 3.2.</p><p>Neuroliberalism began as a Western concept: Isin confined his analysis to ‘Anglophone states’ (2004, p. 218). It has since travelled. By 2014, 136 of the world’s 196 states had adopted behavioural approaches to policy in some form (Whitehead et al., 2014). Its travel is most uneven when it reaches the poor, in Whitehead et al.’s (2019) account the populations of the Global South targeted by development agencies. The World Bank’s (2015) Mind, Society, and Behavior argued that ‘poverty, time pressure, and financial stress all can cause cognitive strain’, citing sugar cane farmers in India who performed worse on thinking tests before harvest than after it (cited in Whitehead et al., 2019, p. 644; Mani et al., 2013). The poor are therefore included on different terms, as ‘marked by distinctive behavioural shortcomings, that are a product of the cognitive toll that impoverishment places upon them’ (p. 643). Poverty is recognised as the cause, yet the intervention targets the mind rather than the poverty, ‘a form of structural adjustment policy operating at a neurological level’ (p. 645). Linking irrationality with ‘local’, ‘traditional’ and ‘indigenous’ places, and so treating the poor as having weaker minds, as colonial rulers once did, is ‘not so much neuroliberal as neurocolonial’ (p. 645). It also ‘denies the possibility that reason may be found in non-market oriented actions (such as reciprocity and care giving)’ (p. 645).</p><p>This matters for mental health, because need is concentrated among the poor. Poverty and common mental disorders are closely linked (Lund et al., 2010), and poverty can cause depression and anxiety (Ridley et al., 2020), yet the poor are least able to reach professional care. In Indonesia, psychiatric care is largely privately insured and some families still contain distress through pasung (Section 1.2). The AI chatbot enters this gap. It can become the care offered to the poor in place of the care, income and security they lack, reaching those whom neuroliberalism already pictures as minds to be managed. Many such apps draw on mindfulness, whose own history shows how this thinking travels.</p><p>Mindfulness has a mixed place in this literature. Whitehead et al. (2016) saw it as a possible way to resist behavioural government, by making visible the ‘political and commercial exploitation of the collective unconscious’ (p. 569, cited in Asker, 2024, p. 3). Yet it has also been absorbed into neuroliberalism. Asker (2024) traces how it was separated from its Buddhist roots and turned into a non-religious therapeutic technique, through Mindfulness-Based Stress Reduction and then Mindfulness-Based Cognitive Therapy, which combined it with CBT (pp. 2–3). In workplaces it shapes the ‘neurocitizen’, ‘skilled in the government of the self, committed to self-optimisation and self-responsibility’ (Pykett and Enright, 2016, cited in Asker, 2024, p. 4). Purser (2019) calls this ‘McMindfulness’, in which ‘the crisis is in the heads of individual people, not in the structures and institutions of society’ (Goto-Jones, 2013, cited in Asker, 2024, p. 4). A practice drawn from Asian meditation traditions now returns to the Global South, including Indonesia, through apps and chatbots. Asker cautions against treating it as wholly captured, however, and points to collective alternatives such as Walsh’s (2018) queer mindfulness: ‘if we are all obsessed with being well individually, we will not be well together’ (cited on p. 5).</p><p>Isin’s (2004) neurotic citizen explains what this government aims at. Anxieties ‘are objects of government not in order to cure or eliminate such states but to manage them’ (p. 225), and neuropolitics is ‘neither focused on causes, nor cure nor care but on tranquillization of anxiety’ (p. 228): it calms people rather than removing what makes them anxious. Of digital networks, he observes that ‘the users of the most rational machine ever invented may have become the most neurotic subjects in history’ (p. 230). Kinnari et al. (2025) apply this to lifelong learning: because the ideal of the ability-capital machine can never be reached, it produces anxious, inadequate citizens who must then manage that anxiety themselves, so that ‘structural issues are treated with individual therapeutic methods’ (p. 15).</p><p>Wellbeing plays three roles in this government. It is where strain is located, as difficulties arising from work, school, family and insecure living conditions appear as individual deficits (Kvist Lindholm and Wickström, 2020). It is the remedy, offered as a competence and a form of capital, increasingly through tools that monitor and nudge individuals (Kotouza et al., 2022). And it is a market, served by wellness industries (Isin, 2004, p. 228) and ‘a massive therapeutic industry’ (Kinnari et al., 2025, p. 13). Kinnari et al. read the result as cruel optimism: ‘a relation of cruel optimism exists when something you desire is actually an obstacle to your flourishing’ (Berlant, 2011, p. 1, cited on p. 15). Relational wellbeing (Section 3.2) names the alternative this hides.</p><p>These accounts link neuroliberalism directly to wellbeing and to an anxious citizen, who closely resembles the chatbot user of Sections 3.1 and 3.3. They also have limits. First, they rest mainly on theory: Isin presents his examples as illustrations of the concept rather than case studies (2004, p. 226), and Kinnari et al. read policy texts without examining how people take these ideals up. Second, they are mostly based on the West. Isin’s analysis is limited to English-speaking countries, and when Whitehead et al. turn to the Global South, it is to show how neuroliberalism represents the poor, not how people there understand themselves. None considers a relational self, or a society in which care is still carried by the family. Third, although Whitehead et al.’s definition includes corporations, their analysis concentrates on public policy (p. 634). Technology companies and news media remain largely unexamined. With AI chatbots, a company designs the choices and the steering happens in a private conversation, and in Indonesia no state framework stands behind it. This raises the question this study pursues: when no one has formally been given the task of governing these conversations, who is understood to be responsible for the wellbeing they produce? Section 3.5 examines how news discourse answers it.</p>"}, {"id": "s3-5", "num": "3.5", "heading": "News discourse and the construction of social problems", "chapter": "Chapter 3 · Literature Review", "kind": "rich", "html": "<p>News does not simply report problems; it helps decide what they are. Hall et al. (1978) showed that journalists rely on ‘primary definers’, officials and experts whose account of an issue sets the terms that everyone else must respond to. As Section 1.5 noted, this makes news a cheap and effective route for those competing to define a problem and its solution (Chermak, 1997). The same is true of wellbeing. Campbell (2024), drawing on Fonagy, found that UK newspapers carried no reports on young people’s mental health in 1990 and only nine in 1995, but 996 in 2015. From around 2015, The Guardian, The Independent and The Times began to describe the issue in ‘alarmist terminology like “crisis” and “epidemic”’ (p. 64). A wellbeing crisis, in other words, can be constructed in the news before it is established anywhere else.</p><p>Problems constructed in the news also follow a life cycle. Downs (1972) described an ‘issue-attention cycle’ in five stages: a pre-problem stage, alarmed discovery and enthusiasm for solutions, a growing realisation of the cost of progress, a gradual decline of public interest, and a post-problem stage. This study adapts that cycle into three stages. In emergence, AI chatbots for wellbeing are discovered and greeted with enthusiasm. In normalisation, they become part of everyday life and their costs become visible. In containment, the question turns to who should control them. Following Downs, these stages are treated as patterns of attention to be traced in the corpus, not as fixed periods (Section 4.3).</p><p>News also decides who must respond to a problem, and increasingly that is the family. Isin (2004) observed that anxious discourse about young people is ‘targeted at those who govern them: parents, guardians, families’ (p. 228), producing anxious parents as well as anxious children. Sointu (2005) found an earlier form of this, in coverage that made women responsible for wellbeing at home (Section 1.6). Family and lifestyle journalism carries this work most directly. Hanusch (2012) describes lifestyle journalism as a distinct field that addresses readers in their everyday lives and offers guidance on how to live them. A column such as Jargon’s ‘Family &amp; Tech’ does not report on regulation. It tells parents what to watch for and what to do.</p><p>These accounts give the two news datasets their questions. RQ1 asks who defines AI chatbot wellbeing in The Guardian, and how that definition shifts across the three stages. RQ2 asks how family journalism relocates responsibility into the household. Neither can be answered by counting. Quantitative content analysis and topic modelling (Tandoc et al., 2025; Sun, Ünlü and Johri, 2026) show which frames appear and how often (Section 1.4), but responsibility is allocated in how a sentence is built: who is made the subject of an action, what is presupposed, and who is never quoted. These features depend on context, a silence leaves nothing to count, and measuring a concept can narrow what it means (High et al., 2023). With a corpus of around 100 articles, the study prioritises depth over breadth, using the qualitative news discourse analysis set out in Chapter 4.</p>"}, {"id": "s4-1", "num": "4.1", "heading": "Research Design", "chapter": "Chapter 4 · Methodology", "kind": "rich", "html": "<p>This study examines how wellbeing has been defined in news discourse about AI chatbots, and who has been made responsible for it, from 2023 to 2026. It uses a qualitative, multi-site design in which each research question is answered with its own dataset. RQ1 examines public news discourse in The Guardian. RQ2 examines family-focused technology journalism, through Julie Jargon’s ‘Family &amp; Tech’ column in The Wall Street Journal. RQ3 examines how those who produce and regulate digital mental health care in Indonesia define wellbeing, responsibility and containment. The two news datasets are analysed with van Dijk’s (1983; 1988) news discourse analysis, and the interviews with reflexive thematic analysis (Braun &amp; Clarke, 2006; 2019). Table 4.1 summarises each dataset with its research question, method, goal and size.</p><p><strong>Table 4.1</strong> Method summary</p><table><thead><tr><th>Dataset</th><th>Research question</th><th>Method</th><th>Goal</th><th>Total</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Public news</td><td>RQ1</td><td>Qualitative; news discourse analysis of articles in The Guardian</td><td>Trace how wellbeing was redefined through AI chatbots across emergence, normalisation and containment</td><td>Guardian articles retrieved with “AI chatbot and wellbeing”, from 1 January 2023 (collection continues to 31 December 2026; 86 identified so far; n after screening: to be confirmed)</td></tr><tr><td>Family journalism</td><td>RQ2</td><td>Qualitative; news discourse analysis of Julie Jargon&#x27;s &quot;Family &amp; Tech&quot; column (The Wall Street Journal)</td><td>Examine how news discourse relocates AI chatbot wellbeing from public regulation into family and private life</td><td>All relevant columns, 20 articles from WSJ site searches, 1 June 2023 to 10 October 2026 (collection continues to 31 December 2026, see Table 4.3)</td></tr><tr><td>Interviews</td><td>RQ3</td><td>Qualitative; semi-structured online interviews, purposive sampling, data triangulation</td><td>Understand how producers and the professional body define wellbeing, responsibility and containment</td><td>Riliv co-founders (2), Riliv psychologists (up to 5), HIMPSI representatives (n: to be confirmed)</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The three datasets are designed to work together. The two news datasets share one analytical protocol (Section 4.3) and overlapping time windows. The Guardian corpus runs from 1 January 2023 to 31 December 2026, and the Jargon corpus from 1 June 2023 to 31 December 2026. Because the two windows cover almost the same period, public news and family journalism can be compared directly at each stage.</p><p>The third dataset moves from Global North news discourse to the Indonesian case in 2026. It is added for data triangulation, which Denzin (1978) defines as the study of one phenomenon through several sources of data, across time, space and persons. The design uses all three dimensions. Across time, the news datasets trace wellbeing from 2023 to 2026, while the interviews capture how it is understood in 2026. Across space, Global North news is set against Indonesia, where AI chatbots are already part of everyday wellbeing practice but no framework yet governs them. Across persons, journalists’ accounts are compared with those of founders, psychologists and a professional body.</p><p>Triangulation is not used here to verify one source against another, a use Fürsich (2009) warns against. Instead, following Liao et al. (2024), who read news frames against user accounts, it is used to show where the meaning of wellbeing converges and where it diverges between sources. Each dataset therefore supplies a perspective the others cannot. The news datasets show how wellbeing is defined in public and domestic discourse, and the interviews show how those who build and regulate care respond to those definitions.</p>"}, {"id": "s4-2", "num": "4.2", "heading": "Why News Discourse", "chapter": "Chapter 4 · Methodology", "kind": "rich", "html": "<p>News is chosen because it is where emerging technologies are first introduced to the public and where their hopes and fears are set out. The study&#x27;s focus is techno-solutionism, which Morozov (2013) describes as recasting complex social conditions as problems with a technological fix. Weiss-Blatt (2021) shows that tech coverage swung from a long period of techno-optimism to a backlash from 2017 that recast Silicon Valley from saviour to threat. This study asks whether that backlash displaced the solutionist premise or only redirected it, in the specific case of AI chatbots and wellbeing after ChatGPT&#x27;s public release: whether wellbeing continues to be framed as a problem that a chatbot, or a better-designed and better-regulated chatbot, can solve.</p><p>The closest recent precedent is Liao et al. (2024), who paired a frame analysis of news coverage of Replika with user interviews (Section 1.4). This study departs from it in two ways. First, it uses news discourse analysis rather than frame analysis, because its interest is in how responsibility for wellbeing is allocated in the text itself: through word choice, agency, choice of sources and what is left unsaid. Second, its interviewees are not users but those who produce and regulate mental health technology, in a context where no framework yet governs AI chatbots.</p><p>Fürsich (2009) justifies treating news texts as data in their own right. She argues that media texts are a distinct stage between how media are made (encoding) and how audiences read them (decoding), and that only textual analysis can show their narrative structure and the values they carry. She also warns against using producer interviews to confirm a reading of the texts. For this reason, the interviews in RQ3 are not used to verify the readings of RQ1 and RQ2. They are a separate site, placed alongside the news analysis so that the two can be compared.</p>"}, {"id": "s4-3", "num": "4.3", "heading": "Analytical Approach: News Discourse Analysis", "chapter": "Chapter 4 · Methodology", "kind": "rich", "html": "<p>Both news datasets are analysed with van Dijk&#x27;s (1983; 1988) news discourse analysis. Unlike quantitative content analysis, it reads beneath manifest content (what a text says openly) to implications, presuppositions (what a text assumes without saying it) and the strategies that remain implicit in the text (van Dijk, 1983). Each article is read at four levels.</p><p>1. Thematic structure. The headline and lead express the main macroproposition, or main point, of a news story (van Dijk, 1983). For each article, the overall topic is derived by deleting detail, generalising and constructing a summary proposition, and any gap between headline and body is noted.</p><p>2. Schematic structure. Each article is mapped onto van Dijk&#x27;s (1983) news superstructure, the standard structure of a news story: summary (headline, lead), episode (main events, background, context, consequences and reactions) and comments (expectations, evaluation). This shows where wellbeing, users and chatbots are placed: as main event, as background, or as reaction.</p><p>3. Local coherence. Relations between sentences, such as adding detail, generalising and contrasting, show how a single case is turned into a general trend.</p><p>4. Style and syntax. Word choice for users, families, chatbots and companies is recorded, along with passive constructions and nominalisations (turning an action into a noun, as in ‘the closure’ rather than ‘the company closed’) that can conceal who acts and who is responsible (Fowler et al., 1979, as cited in van Dijk, 1983). Quoted sources are coded by type, as the choice of sources decides whose account of wellbeing is heard.</p><p>Analysis begins with prolonged reading of the corpus, which Fürsich (2009) describes as the &quot;long preliminary soak&quot; (Hall, 1975, as cited in Fürsich, 2009). Articles are then coded in NVivo across the four levels and compared in sequence. The boundaries between emergence, normalisation and containment are drawn from the corpus rather than fixed in advance.</p>"}, {"id": "s4-4", "num": "4.4", "heading": "Dataset 1 (RQ1): Public News Discourse", "chapter": "Chapter 4 · Methodology", "kind": "rich", "html": "<p>The first dataset is drawn from The Guardian. A single newspaper is chosen over a multi-outlet database such as Nexis, which Liao et al. (2024) used for their Replika corpus, because the analysis prioritises depth over breadth. The Guardian also offers continuity with Sointu (2005), who traced how the same newspaper redefined wellbeing between 1985 and 2003, so the present corpus can be read as a later chapter of that trajectory.</p><p><strong>Table 4.2</strong> Guardian search parameters for Dataset 1</p><table><thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Setting</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Source</td><td>The Guardian</td></tr><tr><td>Search</td><td>“AI chatbot and wellbeing” (86 articles identified so far)</td></tr><tr><td>Language</td><td>English</td></tr><tr><td>Start date</td><td>1 January 2023, a month after the public release of ChatGPT on 30 November 2022 (OpenAI, 2022)</td></tr><tr><td>End date</td><td>31 December 2026 (collection continues)</td></tr><tr><td>Recorded metadata</td><td>Title, date, author, word count</td></tr></tbody></table><p>ChatGPT’s public release on 30 November 2022 marks the moment generative chatbots entered everyday use. The search returned no relevant Guardian articles from the month between that release and the end of 2022, so the corpus begins on 1 January 2023, and emergence is traced from that point. The search for “AI chatbot and wellbeing” has identified 86 articles so far, and will be repeated until 31 December 2026, with new articles added. Screening follows Liao et al. (2024), who reduced 1,008 LexisNexis results on Replika to 104 unique articles by screening for relevance and eliminating duplicates. In the same way, duplicates and updated versions of the same article are removed first, and articles that mention chatbots only in passing, or do not concern wellbeing, are then excluded. Each remaining article is tagged with its metadata (title, date, author, word count) before coding. The final number of articles will be reported after screening, and the full article list will be given in an appendix.</p>"}, {"id": "s4-5", "num": "4.5", "heading": "Dataset 2 (RQ2): Family Journalism", "chapter": "Chapter 4 · Methodology", "kind": "rich", "html": "<p>The second dataset is Julie Jargon&#x27;s &quot;Family &amp; Tech&quot; column in The Wall Street Journal, from 1 June 2023, with collection continuing to 31 December 2026. The column is chosen because it writes about technology from inside the household. Where the Guardian corpus shows AI chatbot wellbeing as a public matter of claims, actors and remedies, the column shows it as something happening to a child or a partner, which a parent or family member must manage.</p><p>Articles are included where they concern AI chatbots in relation to wellbeing, mental health, children, family life, or family decisions such as careers and household tasks. A single columnist gives a consistent writing voice across the period, so shifts in the column can be read as shifts in discourse rather than changes of writer.</p><p>The corpus is retrieved through the WSJ’s own site search. A search for “julie jargon AI chatbot”, filtered to the past year and sorted by relevance, returned 16 Jargon articles published between 11 October 2025 and 26 September 2026. Further searches of the site added three earlier articles, from June 2023 and June 2024, and one published on 10 October 2026, giving 20 articles in total (Table 4.3). Three of them concern AI in careers and household tasks. The search will be repeated until 31 December 2026 and new articles added.</p><p><strong>Table 4.3</strong> Julie Jargon articles on AI chatbot use in the RQ2 corpus (newest first)</p><table><thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>Title</th><th>Topic</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>10 Oct 2026</td><td>OpenAI Makes Progress in Preventing AI-Driven ChatGPT Delusions</td><td>Delusion, company safeguards</td></tr><tr><td>26 Sep 2026</td><td>Middle-Schoolers Are Seeking Out AI Friends Instead of Real Human Ones</td><td>Children, companionship, loneliness</td></tr><tr><td>12 Sep 2026</td><td>A Dead Father, Reincarnated With AI: Is This How We Will Remember Loved Ones Now?</td><td>Grief, chatbot of a dead parent</td></tr><tr><td>18 Aug 2026</td><td>What Parents Need to Know About OpenAI&#x27;s New ChatGPT for Teens</td><td>Parental guidance, teen guardrails</td></tr><tr><td>11 Jul 2026</td><td>How AI Advice Is Undermining Eating-Disorder Therapy</td><td>Eating disorders, therapy</td></tr><tr><td>27 Jun 2026</td><td>The Three Chatbot Behaviors That Can Drive Humans to Delusional Thinking</td><td>Excessive flattery, delusion</td></tr><tr><td>25 May 2026</td><td>His Chatbot Nearly Ruined Him. To Recover, He Had to Destroy It.</td><td>Dependence, recovery</td></tr><tr><td>18 Apr 2026</td><td>For Dementia Patients, AI Can Be a Good, Non-Judgmental Listener</td><td>Dementia, cognitive exercise</td></tr><tr><td>28 Mar 2026</td><td>The People Who Are Using AI at Home to Free Up Their Time</td><td>Household tasks, AI agents</td></tr><tr><td>4 Mar 2026</td><td>Gemini Said They Could Only Be Together if He Killed Himself. Soon, He Was Dead.</td><td>Companion attachment, suicide</td></tr><tr><td>31 Jan 2026</td><td>7 Reasons Teens Say No to AI</td><td>Teens, refusal of AI</td></tr><tr><td>27 Dec 2025</td><td>AI Chatbots Linked to Psychosis, Say Doctors (with Sam Schechner)</td><td>Psychosis, clinical view</td></tr><tr><td>11 Dec 2025</td><td>He Blames ChatGPT for the Murder-Suicide That Shattered His Family</td><td>Family, wrongful death</td></tr><tr><td>22 Nov 2025</td><td>A Tale of Two Sisters: What AI Means for Their Different Career Paths</td><td>Young adults, career security</td></tr><tr><td>6 Nov 2025</td><td>Seven Lawsuits Allege OpenAI Encouraged Suicide, Harmful Delusions (with Sam Schechner)</td><td>Litigation, suicide</td></tr><tr><td>25 Oct 2025</td><td>Afraid to Try AI? These Tech-Savvy Seniors Will Change Your Mind</td><td>Older adults, everyday use</td></tr><tr><td>11 Oct 2025</td><td>Giving Career Advice to Kids Has Never Been Harder</td><td>Parents, teens&#x27; careers</td></tr><tr><td>1 Jun 2024</td><td>AI Fake Nudes Are Now a Frightening Reality for Teens</td><td>Teens, deepfake nudes, schools</td></tr><tr><td>7 Jun 2023</td><td>How a Chatbot Went Rogue</td><td>Chatbot failure, harmful advice</td></tr><tr><td>1 Jun 2023</td><td>A Chatbot Was Designed to Help Prevent Eating Disorders. Then It Gave Dieting Tips.</td><td>Eating disorders, prevention chatbot</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The column is analysed with the same four-level protocol as Dataset 1 (Section 4.3). Because both datasets share a procedure, and the Jargon corpus falls within the Guardian window, the analysis can compare how public news and family journalism allocate responsibility for wellbeing from June 2023 to December 2026.</p>"}, {"id": "s4-6", "num": "4.6", "heading": "Dataset 3 (RQ3): Online Interviews", "chapter": "Chapter 4 · Methodology", "kind": "rich", "html": "<p>The third dataset consists of semi-structured online interviews with people who produce and regulate digital mental health care in Indonesia. These interviews are not fieldwork. RQ1 and RQ2 already rest on large news datasets, so the interviews act as a focused follow-up on how &quot;wellbeing&quot; and techno-solutionism are understood, rather than a study of how things are done in the field. Riliv is the anchor of this dataset. It is an award-winning mental health support app in Indonesia, operating since 2015. It connects the three groups of participants: its founders build the technology, its psychologists deliver care through it, and HIMPSI (Himpunan Psikologi Indonesia, the Indonesian Psychological Association) is both its collaboration partner and the body that regulates its psychologists.</p><p><strong>Table 4.4</strong> Interview participant groups</p><table><thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Participants</th><th>Sampling</th><th>Perspective</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Riliv founders</td><td>2 co-founders</td><td>Purposive</td><td>Production and business: how the platform defines and designs for wellbeing</td></tr><tr><td>Riliv psychologists</td><td>Up to 5 psychologists working on the app, prioritising those who handle family wellbeing cases</td><td>Purposive, by referral</td><td>Clinical practice: where self-governance ends and clinical care begins</td></tr><tr><td>HIMPSI (Indonesian Association of Psychologists)</td><td>Representatives of the Department of National Policy Synergy (Bidang Sinergitas Kebijakan Nasional), reached through the Riliv founders</td><td>Purposive, by referral</td><td>Professional governance: standards, responsibility and what containment would require</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Purposive sampling selects information-rich cases that can speak directly to the research question (Palinkas et al., 2015). Here it is justified because the question concerns a small, specialised group rather than a population. Psychologists and HIMPSI participants are both reached through the co-founders&#x27; referral, since the co-founders know which psychologists handle relevant cases and hold the existing route into the association. Interviews are conducted online through the university&#x27;s Microsoft Teams account, so that recordings and data stay on a secure, institutionally managed platform. They last 45 to 60 minutes, are held in Indonesian or English, and are recorded, transcribed and translated where needed.</p><p>Transcripts are analysed with reflexive thematic analysis (Braun &amp; Clarke, 2006; 2019). Van Dijk&#x27;s news schema is not applied here, because an interview has no headline, lead or episodes; his attention to word choice and agency is kept. Coding in NVivo follows Braun and Clarke&#x27;s (2006) six phases:</p><ul><li>Familiarisation: each transcript is read in full and initial ideas are recorded in memos.</li><li>Coding: transcripts are coded line by line, mostly with codes drawn from the data itself, plus a small set carried over from RQ1 and RQ2 (definitions of wellbeing, allocation of responsibility, self-care versus clinical care, views on containment).</li><li>Generating themes: codes are grouped into candidate themes, with participant group (founder, psychologist, HIMPSI) recorded as a case attribute so groups can be compared.</li><li>Reviewing themes against the full transcripts, not only coded extracts.</li><li>Defining and naming themes.</li><li>Writing up, using short extracts with psychologists identified by initials.</li></ul><p>At phases 2 and 5, each extract is also read for agency: who is positioned as acting, who is acted upon, and who is absent. This mirrors the analysis of agency and choice of sources in the news datasets (Section 4.3) and allows interview themes to be set beside the news findings. Braun and Clarke (2019) treat themes as constructed by the researcher rather than found in the data, so analytic decisions are recorded in memos throughout.</p><p>The study will seek ethical approval from Goldsmiths, University of London before interviews begin. All participants give informed consent. Psychologists are identified by their initials only in transcripts. When they describe client cases, they do not reveal any detail that could identify the client, such as name, age or gender. Because Riliv&#x27;s founders are publicly identifiable, they will be asked separately whether they consent to being named.</p>"}, {"id": "s4-7", "num": "4.7", "heading": "Limitations", "chapter": "Chapter 4 · Methodology", "kind": "rich", "html": "<p>First, the news corpora are English-language and largely Global North, while the interviews are Indonesian. This is a deliberate contrast, but the news cannot stand for Indonesian media discourse. Indonesian news media were considered as a corpus but not used, because their coverage of AI rarely concerns wellbeing. Tuasikal and Gunawan (2024) analysed 777 articles on AI published by five major Indonesian outlets between January and June 2024. Business sources dominated (55.6%), well ahead of government (16.39%) and academics (13%). Most articles (58.9%) concerned the AI industry itself, while 23.5% discussed AI’s impact on society and labour and 16.2% its misuse or regulation. A corpus drawn from Indonesian news would therefore offer too little on AI chatbot wellbeing to answer RQ1 and RQ2. That absence is itself part of the Indonesian case, and the interviews in RQ3 are where it can be explored.</p><p>Second, each news corpus rests on a single title. The Guardian and Jargon’s column offer depth but not breadth, and because one is British and the other American, some differences between them may reflect national context rather than the type of journalism. Third, both corpora depend on keyword searches, which may miss articles that discuss the topic in other terms, such as ‘mental health’ or ‘companion’ (Liao et al., 2024). The Jargon corpus is also uneven over time. Only three of its 20 articles predate October 2025, so it can say little about the emergence stage. Fourth, recruiting psychologists and HIMPSI participants through Riliv’s co-founders, and my own earlier volunteering at Riliv (Section 1.9), may favour voices close to Riliv. Participants may also be reluctant to criticise, given norms of sungkan and hormat. These limits will be stated when reporting findings, and translation from Indonesian will be checked against the original transcripts. Fifth, interviews held by video call on Microsoft Teams depend on participants having a private space in which to speak freely. Where a participant does not have access to a private room at home, I will arrange a private room in their city so that the call can take place comfortably and confidentially. Finally, textual analysis can evaluate only one stop in the circuit of culture, the cycle linking how media are produced, what they say and how they are received (Fürsich, 2009), which is why the interviews are placed beside it rather than used to confirm it.</p>"}, {"id": "s4-8", "num": "4.8", "heading": "Timeline", "chapter": "Chapter 4 · Methodology", "kind": "rich", "html": "<p>The study began in autumn 2024 and is funded by the Indonesia Endowment Fund for Education (LPDP), which pays tuition fees until the end of the fourth year, in September 2028. The plan in Table 4.5 aims to complete data collection and analysis within that funded period. Completing the whole thesis within four years is tight, however. Industrial action is currently in place at Goldsmiths, and it may slow supervision and administrative steps such as ethics approval. A fifth year, from October 2028 to September 2029, is therefore planned as a writing-up year.</p><p><strong>Table 4.5</strong> Research timeline</p><table><thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>Period</th><th>Activities</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Years 1–2</td><td>Oct 2024 – Sep 2026</td><td>Literature review; refining the research questions and design; trial searches of news archives (completed)</td></tr><tr><td>Year 3</td><td>Oct – Dec 2026</td><td>Upgrade; Goldsmiths ethics application; Guardian and Jargon collection continues to 31 December 2026</td></tr><tr><td>Year 3</td><td>Jan – Feb 2027</td><td>Screening and initial coding of both news corpora in NVivo (RQ1, RQ2); finalising the interview guide</td></tr><tr><td>Year 3</td><td>Mar – May 2027</td><td>Online interviews with Riliv founders, Riliv psychologists and HIMPSI (RQ3); transcription and translation</td></tr><tr><td>Year 3</td><td>Jun – Sep 2027</td><td>Full coding of both news corpora; reflexive thematic analysis of interview transcripts</td></tr><tr><td>Year 4</td><td>Oct 2027 – Mar 2028</td><td>Completing the analysis; drafting the news findings chapters</td></tr><tr><td>Year 4</td><td>Apr – Sep 2028</td><td>Drafting the interview findings chapter and the discussion; end of LPDP tuition funding</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Year 5 (writing up)</p><p>Oct 2028 – Sep 2029</p><p>Revising and completing the full thesis; submission and viva</p>"}, {"id": "references", "num": "", "heading": "References", "chapter": "Back matter", "kind": "rich", "html": "<p><em>Add the full reference list here (Goldsmiths Harvard style), one reference per paragraph.</em></p>"}, {"id": "appendices", "num": "", "heading": "Appendices", "chapter": "Back matter", "kind": "rich", "html": "<p><em>Appendix A: Guardian article list. Appendix B: interview and focus group guides. Appendix C: information sheet and consent form.</em></p>"}]};
